<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1770">1770</a>–<a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as G.W.F. Hegel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel was a German philosopher, the leading figure of German idealism after Kant. His works, including the Phenomenology of Spirit (1807) and the Elements of the Philosophy of Right (1820), present history as the development of reason and freedom. From 1818 he taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a>, and his ideas shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and much of nineteenth- and twentieth-century thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georg-wilhelm-friedrich-hegel.jpg" alt="Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jakob Schlesinger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jakob_Schlesinger_-_Hegel_1831.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The reforms after 1806 included Wilhelm von Humboldt’s foundation of the University of Berlin in 1810, which joined teaching to research and was imitated in the United States and elsewhere; Immanuel Kant taught at Königsberg and <b>G.W.F. Hegel</b> in Berlin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel</b>, in his Elements of the Philosophy of Right (1820), argued that a hereditary monarch with limited powers best represented the unity of the state and gave it continuity in emergencies, because a crown that passed by birth was no prize for factions.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.78,9.18&amp;year=1809" aria-label="Stuttgart on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4762 460 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5212 730h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.78,9.18&amp;year=1809">What was here in 1809? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…what experience and history teach is this-that peoples and governments have never learned anything from history, nor have they acted on principles deduced from it.&#8221;</p><footer>Lectures on the Philosophy of History, introduction (1837) &#183; tr. J. Sibree, 1857 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12351/pg12351-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…we may affirm absolutely that nothing great in the world has been accomplished without passion.&#8221;</p><footer>Lectures on the Philosophy of History, introduction (1837) &#183; tr. J. Sibree, 1857 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12351/pg12351-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The history of the world is none other than the progress of the consciousness of freedom…&#8221;</p><footer>Lectures on the Philosophy of History, introduction (1837) &#183; tr. J. Sibree, 1857 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12351/pg12351-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
